t/148-foundation-runner-group-kill.t subtest 1 ("backgrounded sleep is killed by the timeout, not reparented to init") fails on a shared machine for reasons that have nothing to do with karr.

The orphan scan walks all of /proc and flags survivors with an unanchored substring match:

        if ( $cmdline =~ /sleep 30/ ) {
            push @still_running, [ $entry, $cmdline, $ppid ];
        }

That matches any process anywhere on the box whose command line merely contains the text "sleep 30" -- including `sleep 300`, `sleep 3000`, and any shell whose -c string mentions one. Observed here: another agent session was running `sleep 300` in an unrelated repo, and both it and its parent bash were reported as survivors of a group kill they were never part of:

    not ok 3 - no orphan sleep 30 left on the box
    # survivors: 317936//bin/bash -c ... eval sleep 300; cd /home/getty/dev/dbio-dev/... /1558 317940/sleep 300 /317936

Note the test name says "sleep 30" while the survivor is "sleep 300" -- the diag is self-refuting if read closely.

Reproducible while such a process exists (3/3 runs red), and green otherwise, which is why the #171 baseline run saw only t/67 fail. Confirmed independent of #175: fails identically with that change stashed. The other subtest ("runner puts the agent in a process group whose pgid is its own pid") passes throughout, so the product behaviour under test looks fine -- this is the detector, not the detected.

The scan needs to identify the test own child specifically rather than pattern-match the box: anchor on the exact argv (`sleep` + `30` as separate argv entries, cmdline is NUL-separated before the s/\0/ /g), and/or restrict to descendants of the test process / the spawned runner, and/or use a sentinel argument unique to this run instead of a bare duration. Marking it a foundation-runner regression when it fires is a false alarm.
2026-08-15 15:10 Fixed: cmdline =~ /sleep 30/ was an unanchored substring, matching unrelated survivors (e.g. "sleep 300" from a concurrent session) and would also cross-match between two simultaneous copies of this test. Approach: embedded a per-run marker into the sleep duration itself (30.$$, unique to this test process's pid) and switched the final scan from substring to exact argv match (cmdline eq "sleep $marker"). Rejected restricting the scan to descendants of the test process/group: the point of this subtest is to catch a sleep that escaped the group and got reparented to init, so scoping to descendants would blind it to the exact regression it exists to detect. Commit a9a9f52 (only t/148-foundation-runner-group-kill.t touched; left lib/App/karr/Git.pm to #178 and Task.pm to #177). Verification: (1) full suite green in the live tree, 164/164 files, 1105/1105 tests, t/67 included and passing (already fixed by #175); (2) t/148 alone green 3/3 fresh runs while a concurrent "sleep 300" process ran on the box, reproducing the false-alarm condition from the bug report; (3) red-proof: temporarily reduced the runner's group-kill (kill TERM/KILL -pid -> pid) in Foundation/Runner.pm, reran t/148, orphaned marked sleep (reparented to init, ppid=1) was correctly caught as a failure, then restored Runner.pm exactly (md5sum + git diff --stat confirmed no residual change) before committing. No push, no dzil release.
